    "result": {
      "answer": "**Established facts:** Overlapping genes share part of the same DNA sequence, often by using different reading frames, start sites, or transcript boundaries. This lets bacteriophages encode multiple proteins within fewer nucleotides, increasing coding density and reducing noncoding space.\n\n**Interpretation:** Genome overlap is therefore an efficient solution to strong pressure for compact genomes, such as limits on how much DNA fits inside a capsid. The tradeoff is tighter evolutionary constraint: a mutation in an overlapping region may affect more than one gene, limiting how independently the proteins can evolve.",
